 theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,1)$ and $B(1,3).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=x{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,3)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =(1(3)^{2}-{1}^{2})-(0(1)^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =8-0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dbc02597e8ddc550c47f2fc5dacb884f2746f162d67373392f9c3d80c71ffd7f"} {"question" "q-125751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "14"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125760" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=-2xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,0)$ to $(\\sqrt{\\pi },1)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125760" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,0)$ and $B(\\sqrt{\\pi },1).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{\\pi },1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{\\pi })^{2}-(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125760" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dbc02597e8ddc550c47f2fc5dacb884f2746f162d67373392f9c3d80c71ffd7f"} {"question" "q-125760" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125760" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125760" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125757" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=(3y-x)\\,i+3x\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,1)$ to $(2,-2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125757" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,1)$ and $B(2,-2).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=3xy-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,-2)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =(3(2)(-2)-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2})-(3(0)(1)-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-12-2-0 \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125757" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dbc02597e8ddc550c47f2fc5dacb884f2746f162d67373392f9c3d80c71ffd7f"} {"question" "q-125757" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125757" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-14"] ["math" "-15"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-6"] ["math" "-8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125757" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-14"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125665" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)={y}^{2}{e}^{x{y}^{2}}\\,i+2xy{e}^{x{y}^{2}}\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,0)$ to $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125665" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,0)$ and $B(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,2).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y{e}^{x{y}^{2}}(1+x{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={e}^{x{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{2}}-{e}^{0} \\\\  & ={e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1 \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{2}^{4}}-1 \\\\  & ={2}^{4}-1 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125665" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dbc02597e8ddc550c47f2fc5dacb884f2746f162d67373392f9c3d80c71ffd7f"} {"question" "q-125665" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125665" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "15"] ["math" "16"] ["math" "32"] ["math" "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2"] ["math" "\\sqrt{e}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125665" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "15"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨y,\\,{x}^{2}⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the curve $y={x}^{3},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{5}{6},$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path.\n- $F$ is conservative." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Parameterizing the curve, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨t,\\,{t}^{3}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨{t}^{3},\\,{t}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{3}+3{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{3{t}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3}{5}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨t,\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t+{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement IV is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dbc02597e8ddc550c47f2fc5dacb884f2746f162d67373392f9c3d80c71ffd7f"} {"question" "q-125791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, III, and IV only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "II, III, and IV only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $F$ is conservative.\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=1,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125723" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dbc02597e8ddc550c47f2fc5dacb884f2746f162d67373392f9c3d80c71ffd7f"} {"question" "q-125723" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125723" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125723" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125792" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨1,\\,x⟩$ and the points $A(1,0)$ and $B(e,1)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2(e-1),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2(e-1),$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the segment line $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path.\n- $F$ is conservative." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125792" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Parameterizing the curve, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨t,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [1,e].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}⟨1,\\,t⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}1+1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2t\\,{|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =2(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨t(e-1)+1,\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨1,\\,t(e-1)+1⟩\\cdot ⟨e-1,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}e-1+t(e-1)+1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t(e-1)+e\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}(e-1)}{2}+et]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{e-1}{2}+e \\\\  & =\\frac{3e-1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement IV is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125792" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dbc02597e8ddc550c47f2fc5dacb884f2746f162d67373392f9c3d80c71ffd7f"} {"question" "q-125792" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125792" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "All the statements are true"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, III and IV only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125792" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125789" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\,1⟩$ and the points $A(1,0)$ and $B(0,1)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ traversed from $A$ to $B$ in counterclockwise direction.\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{1}{3},$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path.\n- $F$ is conservative." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125789" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Parameterizing the circle, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}⟨{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}⟨1,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨1-t,\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨(1-t)^{2}+{t}^{2},\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨2{t}^{2}-2t+1,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2t-2{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[{t}^{2}-\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement IV is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125789" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dbc02597e8ddc550c47f2fc5dacb884f2746f162d67373392f9c3d80c71ffd7f"} {"question" "q-125789" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/q