hree variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨t,\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨t,\\,-t⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t-t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨{t}^{2},\\,-t⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,2t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}-2{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement IV is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125636" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dbc02597e8ddc550c47f2fc5dacb884f2746f162d67373392f9c3d80c71ffd7f"} {"question" "q-125636" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125636" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "All the statements are true."] ["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III and IV only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "q-125636" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/state.json" "file_sha256" "e7ed7362be958fc8e01bbeb16929bf271dc64063f89520a1d716303069ba74f8"} {"question" "e-9548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 + z^2.$ Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int_C \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r},$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-2, 1, 0)$ to $(-1, 0, -1)$ in $\\mathbb R^3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/example-9548.json" "file_sha256" "b9e6dc622e792e40e697251e7bbec6358704461229ee60cb25c2add53e8b7cdd"} {"question" "e-9548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t \\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-2, 1, 0)$ and $B(-1, 0, -1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(-1,0,-1)-f(-2,1,0) \\\\  & =((-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2})-((-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =2-5 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/example-9548.json" "file_sha256" "b9e6dc622e792e40e697251e7bbec6358704461229ee60cb25c2add53e8b7cdd"} {"question" "e-9549" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x, y) = -2x\\,\\mathbf i + 6y^2\\,\\mathbf j.$ Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r},$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-2, 0)$ to $(1, 2)$ in $\\mathbb R^2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/example-9549.json" "file_sha256" "15692a3a6e92d64737ee0a3124d8bf6298a1cd56caac711d07066568869e51b4"} {"question" "e-9549" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t \\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-2,0)$ and $B(1, 2).$\n\nNotice that $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 0.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2,$ we conclude that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf = 2y^3 - x^2.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,2)-f(-2,0) \\\\  & =(2(2)^{3}-{1}^{2})-(2(0)^{3}-(-2)^{2}) \\\\  & =16-1+4 \\\\  & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/example-9549.json" "file_sha256" "15692a3a6e92d64737ee0a3124d8bf6298a1cd56caac711d07066568869e51b4"} {"question" "e-8753" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) =  \\left\\langle ye^{xy},\\, xe^{xy}\\right\\rangle$ and the points $A(1,1)$ and $B(2,2)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = e^4-e,$ where $C_1$ is the path along the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = e^4-e,$ where $C_2$ is the path along along the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/example-8753.json" "file_sha256" "9a46a509368263123f56352fdfbfa2b75f530b8d3eb4da6aabf1403c89ef4886"} {"question" "e-8753" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = (1+xy)e^{xy}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2,$ we conclude that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf F.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf = e^{xy}.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,2)-f(1,1) \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\cdot 2}-{e}^{1\\cdot 1} \\\\  & ={e}^{4}-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\mathbf F$ is conservative and $C_2$ has the same initial and terminal points as $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = \\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = e^{4}-e.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14059496/example-8753.json" "file_sha256" "9a46a509368263123f56352fdfbfa2b75f530b8d3eb4da6aabf1403c89ef4886"}] "usage" {"q-125636" [] "q-125665" [] "q-125760" [] "e-9549" [] "e-9548" [] "e-8753" []} "basis" 1899 "retractions" [[17592186172235 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x, y) =-2xy\\sin x^{2}i + \\cos x^{2}j$. Calculate $∫_{C}F \\cdot dr$, where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0, 0)$ to $(\\sqrt{π}, 1)$ in $R^{2}$."] [17592186172233 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x, y) = y^{2}e^{xy^{2}}i + 2xye^{xy^{2}}j$. Calculate $∫_{C}F \\cdot dr$, where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0, 0)$ to $(\\ln 2, 2)$ in $R^{2}$."] [17592186172231 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x, y) = ⟨y,-x⟩$ and the points $O(0, 0)$ and $A(1, 1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $∫_(C_{1})F \\cdot dr = 0$, where $C_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $OA^(―)$, traversed from $O$ to $A$.\n2. $∫_(C_{2})F \\cdot dr = 0$, where $C_{2}$ is the path along the parabola $y = x^{2}$, traversed from $O$ to $A$.\n3. $∫_(C)F \\cdot dr$ is independent of path.\n4. $F$ is conservative."] [17592186171835 :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 + z^2$. Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int_C \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r}$, where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-2, 1, 0)$ to $(-1, 0, -1)$ in $\\mathbb R^3$."] [17592186171835 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t \\in [a,b]$. Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C$. The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a))\n$$\n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B$, and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-2, 1, 0)$ and $B(-1, 0, -1)$.\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{C}∇f \\cdot dr &= f(-1, 0,-1) - f(-2, 1, 0) \\\\\n&= ((-1)^{2} + 0^{2} + (-1)^{2}) - ((-2)^{2} + 1^{2} + 0^{2}) \\\\\n&= 2 - 5 \\\\\n&=-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186171836 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x, y) = -2x\\,\\mathbf i + 6y^2\\,\\mathbf j$. Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r}$, where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-2, 0)$ to $(1, 2)$ in $\\mathbb R^2$."] [17592186171836 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t \\in [a,b]$. Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C$. The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a))\n$$\n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B$, and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-2,0)$ and $B(1, 2)$.\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nis a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ with domain\n\n$$\nD = \\mathbb R^2\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 0\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2$, we conclude that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that\n\n$$\n\\nabla f = \\mathbf F\n$$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf = 2y^3 - x^2\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{C}F \\cdot dr &= ∫_{C}∇f \\cdot dr \\\\\n&= f(1, 2) - f(-2, 0) \\\\\n&= (2(2)^{3} - 1^{2}) - (2(0)^{3} - (-2)^{2}) \\\\\n&= 16 - 1 + 4 \\\\\n&= 19\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186171793 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\left\\langle ye^{xy},\\, xe^{xy}\\right\\rangle$ and the points $A(1,1)$ and $B(2,2)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.\n2. $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = e^4-e$, where $C_1$ is the path along the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x}$, traversed from $A$ to $B$.\n3. $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = e^4-e$, where $C_2$ is the path along along the line segment $\\overline{AB}$, traversed from $A$ to $B$."] [17592186171793 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b]$. Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C$. The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a))\n$$\n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B$, and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = (1+xy)e^{xy}$.\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2$, we conclude that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf F$.\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf F$. Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n$f = e^{xy}$.\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n$∫_(C_{1})F \\cdot dr|= ∫_(C_{1})∇f \\cdot dr; = f(2, 2) - f(1, 1); = e^{2 \\cdot 2} - e^{1 \\cdot 1}; = e^{4} - e$.\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\mathbf F$ is conservative and $C_2$ has the same initial and terminal points as $C_1$, we have\n$\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = \\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = e^{4}-e$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""]] "immutable_answers" [17592186168590 17592186168591 17592186168592 17592186168593 17592186168594 17592186168602 17592186168603 17592186168604 17592186168605 17592186168606 17592186168614 17592186168615 17592186168616 17592186168617 17592186168618] "immutable_fields" [[17592186168589 :answer-field/correct] [17592186168589 :answer-field/id] [17592186168589 :answer-field/key] [17592186168589 :answer-field/type] [17592186168601 :answer-field/correct] [17592186168601 :answer-field/id] [17592186168601 :answer-field/key] [17592186168601 :answer-field/type] [17592186168613 :answer-field/correct] [17592186168613 :answer-field/id] [17592186168613 :answer-field/key] [17592186168613 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
